From: Lume Retro OS
Subject: [PATCH] audio: fixed 3 dB headroom under the volume setting

On the GA36 the sound of a game was harsh on loud passages through the
speaker, with the volume at 100% (0 dB): at full scale the resampler's
overshoot is clipped in the float to integer conversion, and the console's
small speaker amplifier is driven to its limit.

The gain applied to the core's audio is now the volume setting minus a
fixed 3 dB headroom, in audio_set_float, the one place every volume change
goes through (config load, menu, volume keys). The user's scale does not
change (100% is still the top); the loudest output is 3 dB lower and stays
clean.

--- a/audio/audio_driver.c
+++ b/audio/audio_driver.c
@@ -86,6 +86,9 @@
  /* Converts decibels to voltage gain. Returns voltage gain value. */
 #define DB_TO_GAIN(db) (powf(10.0f, (db) / 20.0f))

+/* Lume: headroom applied under every volume setting (see audio_set_float) */
+#define LUME_AUDIO_HEADROOM_DB (-3.0f)
+
 audio_driver_t audio_null = {
    NULL, /* init */
    NULL, /* write */
@@ -1796,7 +1799,10 @@
    switch (action)
    {
       case AUDIO_ACTION_VOLUME_GAIN:
-         audio_driver_st.volume_gain        = DB_TO_GAIN(val);
+         /* Lume: fixed headroom below the user volume, so full volume
+          * (0 dB) still leaves room for resampler overshoot and does not
+          * overdrive the console's small speaker amplifier. */
+         audio_driver_st.volume_gain        = DB_TO_GAIN(val + LUME_AUDIO_HEADROOM_DB);
          break;
       case AUDIO_ACTION_MIXER_VOLUME_GAIN:
 #ifdef HAVE_AUDIOMIXER
